WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:08.119
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:10.009
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.949
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.959 --> 00:00:14.719
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:17.249
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:19.179
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:20.769
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:24.469
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are going

00:00:24.479 --> 00:00:27.222
to be talking about Operation Bernhard,

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:30.369
and the Nazi plan to bankrupt Britain.

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:33.859
It is the story of a plan to destroy

00:00:33.869 --> 00:00:36.059
the British pound that turned into

00:00:36.059 --> 00:00:38.569
something else entirely: the biggest

00:00:38.779 --> 00:00:40.769
counterfeiting operation in history.

00:00:41.429 --> 00:00:43.709
It is the story of the men who were

00:00:43.709 --> 00:00:46.419
forced to run it, and of the strange

00:00:46.419 --> 00:00:48.709
fact that the better they did their job,

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:51.149
the closer they came to being killed.

00:00:51.679 --> 00:00:53.749
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:53.759 --> 00:00:56.349
minute and get right into it.

00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:01.389
In the mountains of central Austria,

00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:04.439
about an hour's drive east of Salzburg,

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:08.359
there is a small lake called Lake Toplitz.

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:11.409
It is not a particularly pretty

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:13.409
lake, at least not in the way that

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:15.009
most Austrian lakes are pretty.

00:01:15.459 --> 00:01:18.739
It is narrow and dark, squeezed

00:01:18.779 --> 00:01:20.619
between steep cliffs, and

00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:23.289
the water is almost black.

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:26.249
It is also a very strange lake.

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:29.269
It gets to just over 100 metres

00:01:29.269 --> 00:01:32.419
deep, but below about twenty metres,

00:01:32.709 --> 00:01:34.509
there is no oxygen in the water,

00:01:34.769 --> 00:01:37.119
so fish cannot survive down there.

00:01:38.239 --> 00:01:41.189
And halfway down, there is a layer of

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:43.579
sunken tree trunks, floating in the

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:45.789
dark, which makes diving through it

00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:48.789
dangerous, and in places impossible.

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:54.149
But in the summer of 1959, a team

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:56.469
of divers arrived at the lake.

00:01:57.189 --> 00:01:59.749
They had been sent by Stern, a

00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:02.749
German news magazine, and they

00:02:02.749 --> 00:02:04.009
were looking for something.

00:02:05.629 --> 00:02:06.809
From the bottom of the

00:02:06.839 --> 00:02:09.059
lake, they pulled up boxes.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:12.349
Wooden and metal boxes, sealed

00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:15.079
shut, and inside them, packed

00:02:15.229 --> 00:02:18.139
tightly together, were banknotes.

00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:20.843
Bank of England banknotes.

00:02:21.229 --> 00:02:22.819
Five pounds, ten pounds,

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:24.479
twenty pounds, fifty pounds.

00:02:24.869 --> 00:02:27.149
Millions of pounds' worth, and

00:02:27.419 --> 00:02:29.899
despite having been kept underwater

00:02:30.119 --> 00:02:32.829
in this gloomy lake, many of them

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:35.119
were still perfectly legible.

00:02:36.339 --> 00:02:37.909
There was just one problem

00:02:38.199 --> 00:02:39.369
with this treasure.

00:02:40.179 --> 00:02:43.279
Not a single note was real.

00:02:44.179 --> 00:02:46.389
So, today, we are going to

00:02:46.389 --> 00:02:48.459
find out where they came from.

00:02:48.829 --> 00:02:50.719
Who made these notes?

00:02:50.939 --> 00:02:52.259
How did they make them?

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:53.849
What were they used for?

00:02:54.149 --> 00:02:56.709
And why did they end up at the

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.149
bottom of a lake in Austria?

00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:02.559
Let's start at the beginning, which,

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:06.172
for this story, is September 1939.

00:03:07.032 --> 00:03:09.799
Germany has just invaded Poland.

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:12.019
Britain has declared war.

00:03:12.509 --> 00:03:14.799
World War II has started.

00:03:16.199 --> 00:03:19.269
Barely two weeks in, a man called

00:03:19.329 --> 00:03:22.139
Arthur Nebe put forward an idea.

00:03:23.109 --> 00:03:26.249
Nebe was then the head of the German

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:29.739
criminal police, and his idea was simple.

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:33.889
Germany should print vast quantities of

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:37.929
fake British banknotes, load them into

00:03:37.979 --> 00:03:41.569
aircraft, and drop them all over Britain.

00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:44.609
The logic went like this.

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:47.189
If the streets were suddenly full

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:49.449
of money, people would pick it up.

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:50.819
They would spend it.

00:03:51.059 --> 00:03:52.829
Shops would accept it.

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:56.099
And when it became clear that nobody

00:03:56.299 --> 00:03:58.459
could tell the real notes from

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:01.299
the fake ones, people would stop

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:03.339
trusting paper money altogether.

00:04:03.899 --> 00:04:05.599
Prices would shoot up.

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:07.609
The pound would collapse.

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:09.839
And Britain would be beaten

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:12.329
without a single German soldier

00:04:12.449 --> 00:04:15.029
ever setting foot on British soil.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:19.569
It was a neat idea in theory,

00:04:19.809 --> 00:04:22.829
but not everyone was convinced.

00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:25.319
Joseph Goebbels called it a

00:04:25.329 --> 00:04:28.269
grotesque plan, and the economics

00:04:28.269 --> 00:04:30.469
minister objected on the grounds

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.219
that it broke international law.

00:04:32.989 --> 00:04:36.379
But Hitler said yes and ordered

00:04:36.649 --> 00:04:38.869
for the plan to be put in motion.

00:04:39.829 --> 00:04:42.309
It was given a name, Operation

00:04:42.359 --> 00:04:44.969
Andreas, and put in the hands of

00:04:44.969 --> 00:04:46.489
a man called  Alfred Naujocks.

00:04:48.736 --> 00:04:51.238
Naujocks was an SS officer

00:04:51.528 --> 00:04:53.798
with a background in this kind

00:04:53.798 --> 00:04:55.928
of work, covert operations.

00:04:56.978 --> 00:04:59.398
A few weeks earlier, he had staged a

00:04:59.408 --> 00:05:02.388
fake Polish attack on a German radio

00:05:02.388 --> 00:05:05.198
station near the border, with SS men

00:05:05.398 --> 00:05:07.948
dressed in Polish uniforms, which Hitler

00:05:07.948 --> 00:05:10.558
then used as his excuse to invade.

00:05:11.098 --> 00:05:13.578
So faking things was right in

00:05:13.578 --> 00:05:15.208
his wheelhouse, it was something

00:05:15.368 --> 00:05:17.298
he was not unfamiliar with.

00:05:18.238 --> 00:05:20.198
Faking a British banknote,

00:05:20.208 --> 00:05:22.448
though, turned out to be a lot

00:05:22.468 --> 00:05:24.778
harder than faking an attack.

00:05:26.028 --> 00:05:28.158
To understand why, you need

00:05:28.158 --> 00:05:29.648
to picture what a British

00:05:29.648 --> 00:05:32.928
banknote looked like in 1939.

00:05:33.512 --> 00:05:36.252
It was very different from the colorful

00:05:36.512 --> 00:05:38.932
double-sided banknotes you find today.

00:05:39.685 --> 00:05:42.075
A five-pound note was a large

00:05:42.305 --> 00:05:44.795
sheet of white paper, printed

00:05:44.805 --> 00:05:47.625
in black ink on one side only.

00:05:48.385 --> 00:05:50.315
There was no picture of the King,

00:05:50.325 --> 00:05:53.195
no colour, no complicated pattern.

00:05:53.525 --> 00:05:56.785
Just words, a number, and in the top

00:05:56.805 --> 00:06:00.545
left corner, a picture of Britannia, the

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:03.045
female figure who represents Britain,

00:06:03.415 --> 00:06:05.705
sitting with her shield and spear.

00:06:06.715 --> 00:06:07.815
It looked simple.

00:06:08.265 --> 00:06:10.905
And that was the trick, because every part

00:06:10.945 --> 00:06:13.715
of it was fiendishly difficult to copy.

00:06:15.025 --> 00:06:18.045
The Germans found three problems.

00:06:18.705 --> 00:06:20.955
The first was the paper.

00:06:21.825 --> 00:06:23.875
Bank of England notes were made

00:06:23.885 --> 00:06:25.905
from linen and cotton, with

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:27.855
no wood pulp in them at all.

00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:30.835
They had a particular feel, a

00:06:30.945 --> 00:06:32.635
particular sound when you crinkled

00:06:32.635 --> 00:06:35.425
them, and under ultraviolet light

00:06:35.735 --> 00:06:37.985
they glowed in a particular way.

00:06:39.185 --> 00:06:41.675
The first German attempts glowed

00:06:41.695 --> 00:06:44.315
wrong, and any bank clerk in Britain

00:06:44.605 --> 00:06:46.255
would have spotted them instantly.

00:06:47.585 --> 00:06:49.975
The second problem was Britannia,

00:06:50.195 --> 00:06:52.555
the picture on the left-hand side.

00:06:53.395 --> 00:06:56.295
She was engraved in such fine detail

00:06:56.615 --> 00:06:58.745
that the German engravers, who were

00:06:58.745 --> 00:07:00.945
among the best in the world, spent

00:07:00.955 --> 00:07:02.914
seven months trying to copy her.

00:07:03.385 --> 00:07:05.985
And even when they were convinced their

00:07:05.985 --> 00:07:08.545
copy was perfect, some people said

00:07:08.555 --> 00:07:10.355
the eyes were still slightly wrong.

00:07:12.085 --> 00:07:14.845
The third problem was the numbers.

00:07:15.365 --> 00:07:18.315
Every note had a serial number and the

00:07:18.355 --> 00:07:21.005
printed signature of the Chief Cashier,

00:07:21.265 --> 00:07:23.785
and the Bank of England kept a record

00:07:23.945 --> 00:07:26.155
of every number it had ever issued.

00:07:27.125 --> 00:07:29.285
When a note came back to the Bank of

00:07:29.285 --> 00:07:32.435
England in London, a clerk looked it up

00:07:32.595 --> 00:07:35.055
in a huge leather-bound ledger, a kind

00:07:35.055 --> 00:07:38.325
of accounts book, and marked it as paid.

00:07:38.985 --> 00:07:40.955
So a forger couldn't

00:07:40.955 --> 00:07:42.495
simply invent numbers.

00:07:42.755 --> 00:07:44.545
They had to be numbers the Bank

00:07:44.555 --> 00:07:47.105
would recognise, and numbers that

00:07:47.105 --> 00:07:49.135
were not already marked as paid.

00:07:50.466 --> 00:07:52.566
The Germans worked away at

00:07:52.566 --> 00:07:54.776
this for about two years.

00:07:55.306 --> 00:07:57.586
They printed some notes, but the

00:07:57.686 --> 00:08:00.426
quality was patchy, and the man in

00:08:00.426 --> 00:08:02.516
charge of the operation, Naujocks,

00:08:02.876 --> 00:08:04.586
fell out with his superiors.

00:08:05.276 --> 00:08:08.066
By early 1942, Operation

00:08:08.096 --> 00:08:10.826
Andreas was quietly shut down.

00:08:11.586 --> 00:08:14.236
But later that year, in July

00:08:14.246 --> 00:08:18.646
1942, the SS decided to try again.

00:08:19.636 --> 00:08:21.656
This time, the man in charge

00:08:21.786 --> 00:08:25.086
was an SS Major called Bernhard

00:08:25.176 --> 00:08:28.786
Krüger, and the operation was named

00:08:28.816 --> 00:08:31.090
after him: Operation Bernhard.

00:08:32.256 --> 00:08:34.596
It is the name that history remembers for

00:08:34.606 --> 00:08:37.176
the whole story, though strictly speaking

00:08:37.186 --> 00:08:39.038
it was only the second half of it.

00:08:40.138 --> 00:08:43.178
The mechanism was the same, make

00:08:43.258 --> 00:08:45.878
fake pounds, but the purpose

00:08:45.928 --> 00:08:48.578
for doing so was very different.

00:08:49.648 --> 00:08:53.608
By 1942, the world had changed.

00:08:53.968 --> 00:08:56.248
Germany had lost the Battle of

00:08:56.278 --> 00:08:58.918
Britain, and the British controlled

00:08:58.928 --> 00:09:01.458
the airspace over their own island.

00:09:02.158 --> 00:09:05.068
And even if the Germans could somehow

00:09:05.308 --> 00:09:07.598
drop millions of pounds down on England,

00:09:07.938 --> 00:09:09.628
the government would know within

00:09:09.628 --> 00:09:12.068
hours, announce it on the radio, and

00:09:12.078 --> 00:09:14.448
within days every high-value note in

00:09:14.448 --> 00:09:16.268
the country would be called in and

00:09:16.268 --> 00:09:18.728
checked, one serial number at a time.

00:09:20.018 --> 00:09:22.318
And Germany, strangely enough,

00:09:22.618 --> 00:09:25.008
needed British pounds for herself.

00:09:25.778 --> 00:09:29.218
The SS had an extensive spy network across

00:09:29.218 --> 00:09:31.928
Europe, buying information and weapons,

00:09:32.178 --> 00:09:34.558
and given that the German currency

00:09:34.778 --> 00:09:37.238
was largely worthless internationally

00:09:37.448 --> 00:09:40.018
and tightly restricted, it tended to

00:09:40.018 --> 00:09:43.048
pay overseas spies and informants in

00:09:43.088 --> 00:09:46.268
foreign currency: pounds and dollars.

00:09:46.938 --> 00:09:50.258
So the new purpose of the operation was

00:09:50.258 --> 00:09:53.408
to print money that Germany could spend.

00:09:54.869 --> 00:09:57.519
Krüger also had a different idea

00:09:57.769 --> 00:09:59.809
about who should do the printing.

00:10:01.159 --> 00:10:02.699
His predecessors had

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:04.449
used German technicians.

00:10:05.179 --> 00:10:07.849
Krüger went looking somewhere else.

00:10:08.809 --> 00:10:10.369
He toured the concentration

00:10:10.369 --> 00:10:13.199
camps, and he asked for lists.

00:10:13.849 --> 00:10:16.814
He wanted printers, engravers,

00:10:17.072 --> 00:10:18.729
typesetters, paper makers,

00:10:18.779 --> 00:10:21.449
bankers, anyone who had worked with

00:10:21.459 --> 00:10:23.709
money or with printing presses.

00:10:24.809 --> 00:10:26.919
On Himmler’s orders, they were

00:10:26.969 --> 00:10:29.299
all to be Jewish prisoners.

00:10:30.289 --> 00:10:32.629
They could be kept completely isolated,

00:10:32.919 --> 00:10:36.569
and the SS intended to kill them once

00:10:36.569 --> 00:10:38.459
they were no longer useful, leaving

00:10:38.459 --> 00:10:41.099
nobody alive to reveal the operation.

00:10:41.929 --> 00:10:45.629
In September 1942, the first twenty-six

00:10:45.629 --> 00:10:47.959
of them arrived at Sachsenhausen

00:10:47.999 --> 00:10:50.899
concentration camp, just north of Berlin.

00:10:52.019 --> 00:10:54.839
Over the following months, more arrived,

00:10:54.979 --> 00:10:57.879
and by the end there were around a hundred

00:10:57.909 --> 00:11:00.359
and forty men working in the operation.

00:11:01.299 --> 00:11:04.779
They were put in two barracks, Block 18

00:11:05.009 --> 00:11:07.819
and Block 19, at the edge of the camp.

00:11:08.739 --> 00:11:10.569
Around these two blocks, the

00:11:10.579 --> 00:11:13.429
SS built a second fence, with

00:11:13.649 --> 00:11:16.009
barbed wire and its own guards.

00:11:16.489 --> 00:11:18.439
Nobody else in the camp

00:11:18.599 --> 00:11:19.649
was to be allowed in.

00:11:20.529 --> 00:11:23.509
And inside that fence, the prisoners

00:11:23.749 --> 00:11:25.949
found something they had not expected.

00:11:26.539 --> 00:11:28.539
They found decent food.

00:11:28.929 --> 00:11:30.389
More of it than anyone else

00:11:30.389 --> 00:11:31.769
in the camp got, at any rate.

00:11:32.339 --> 00:11:34.649
They found cigarettes and newspapers.

00:11:35.009 --> 00:11:35.899
They found a radio.

00:11:36.309 --> 00:11:37.519
There was a ping-pong table,

00:11:37.829 --> 00:11:39.499
and the prisoners sometimes

00:11:39.499 --> 00:11:40.919
played against the guards.

00:11:42.169 --> 00:11:44.059
The men were not beaten.

00:11:44.729 --> 00:11:47.649
Krüger addressed them as "Sie", the

00:11:47.829 --> 00:11:50.599
polite, formal form of "you" in German,

00:11:50.819 --> 00:11:53.439
rather than the "du" that guards would

00:11:53.449 --> 00:11:55.149
usually use to talk to prisoners.

00:11:55.829 --> 00:11:58.019
He arranged for musicians to visit.

00:11:58.479 --> 00:11:59.959
There were even amateur

00:12:00.009 --> 00:12:01.149
theatre performances.

00:12:01.989 --> 00:12:04.319
To a man who had come from Auschwitz,

00:12:04.529 --> 00:12:06.659
it must have felt like another planet.

00:12:07.969 --> 00:12:10.759
They must surely have known what it meant.

00:12:11.239 --> 00:12:13.079
They were being kept alive and

00:12:13.079 --> 00:12:15.459
treated well because they were useful.

00:12:15.989 --> 00:12:18.719
The moment they stopped being useful or

00:12:18.929 --> 00:12:21.149
the moment the operation ended, well,

00:12:21.489 --> 00:12:23.629
they probably knew what fate awaited them.

00:12:24.599 --> 00:12:27.679
So, what did the work actually look like?

00:12:28.219 --> 00:12:32.479
Well, by late 1942, the three principal

00:12:32.489 --> 00:12:34.539
problems with counterfeiting the pound,

00:12:34.729 --> 00:12:37.299
the manufacturing of paper, the copying

00:12:37.299 --> 00:12:39.539
of Britannia, and the numbering,

00:12:39.959 --> 00:12:41.979
they had all largely been cracked.

00:12:42.729 --> 00:12:45.129
Now, the real work could start.

00:12:46.169 --> 00:12:48.429
The paper came in from a German

00:12:48.429 --> 00:12:50.809
mill called Hahnemühle in big

00:12:50.809 --> 00:12:52.909
sheets, four notes to a sheet.

00:12:53.889 --> 00:12:56.509
The prisoners printed them on six

00:12:56.569 --> 00:12:59.229
presses, checked every one against a

00:12:59.229 --> 00:13:01.499
real note under a magnifying glass,

00:13:01.879 --> 00:13:03.679
added the serial numbers and the

00:13:03.679 --> 00:13:06.309
cashier's signature, and cut them up.

00:13:06.869 --> 00:13:09.929
But a freshly printed banknote was

00:13:09.939 --> 00:13:12.709
just the start; it wasn't "real money".

00:13:13.459 --> 00:13:15.919
A brand-new note is suspicious.

00:13:16.539 --> 00:13:18.899
Real money has been in wallets and

00:13:18.959 --> 00:13:21.259
pockets and been dropped in puddles.

00:13:21.939 --> 00:13:24.249
So, forty or fifty prisoners

00:13:24.479 --> 00:13:26.449
spent their days doing nothing

00:13:26.699 --> 00:13:29.579
but making new notes look old.

00:13:30.099 --> 00:13:32.759
They folded them and unfolded them.

00:13:33.009 --> 00:13:34.449
They rubbed dirt into them.

00:13:34.859 --> 00:13:37.219
They passed them from hand to hand

00:13:37.399 --> 00:13:39.189
until they were soft with sweat.

00:13:39.889 --> 00:13:42.639
Some were pinned together in bundles,

00:13:42.669 --> 00:13:44.589
the way British bank clerks did,

00:13:44.959 --> 00:13:47.159
and then unpinned, leaving the right

00:13:47.199 --> 00:13:49.109
little holes in the right place.

00:13:49.849 --> 00:13:52.739
Then every note was inspected,

00:13:52.909 --> 00:13:55.149
and sorted into grades.

00:13:56.269 --> 00:13:59.239
The best ones, the ones no expert could

00:13:59.239 --> 00:14:02.929
fault, were set aside for paying spies and

00:14:02.929 --> 00:14:05.079
for buying things in neutral countries,

00:14:05.209 --> 00:14:07.729
where the notes would be examined closely.

00:14:08.269 --> 00:14:11.014
The second grade went to collaborators

00:14:11.209 --> 00:14:13.639
and agents in occupied countries.

00:14:14.389 --> 00:14:17.749
A third grade had small flaws, and

00:14:17.909 --> 00:14:20.229
was kept in case anyone ever did

00:14:20.229 --> 00:14:21.839
want to drop money out of aeroplanes.

00:14:22.479 --> 00:14:24.429
And the worst were destroyed.

00:14:25.739 --> 00:14:29.869
At its peak, in 1943 and 1944,

00:14:30.359 --> 00:14:32.099
the workshop was producing

00:14:32.099 --> 00:14:34.019
several hundred thousand notes a

00:14:34.019 --> 00:14:36.619
month, on six printing presses.

00:14:37.679 --> 00:14:40.469
In total, the prisoners printed just

00:14:40.509 --> 00:14:43.926
under nine million banknotes, with a face

00:14:43.926 --> 00:14:48.219
value of more than 130 million pounds.

00:14:49.483 --> 00:14:51.343
To give you some idea of what that

00:14:51.383 --> 00:14:54.643
meant, the total value of Bank of

00:14:54.643 --> 00:14:56.333
England notes in circulation at the

00:14:56.333 --> 00:14:59.463
time was around a billion pounds.

00:15:00.013 --> 00:15:02.703
So a hundred and thirty million pounds

00:15:02.893 --> 00:15:06.093
was more than a tenth of all the paper

00:15:06.153 --> 00:15:08.963
money in Britain, produced by a hundred

00:15:08.963 --> 00:15:11.603
and forty men in two wooden huts.

00:15:12.873 --> 00:15:15.683
Now, printing money is one thing.

00:15:16.213 --> 00:15:17.933
Spending it is another.

00:15:18.853 --> 00:15:20.613
The man in charge of that was

00:15:20.613 --> 00:15:22.653
a businessman, smuggler and

00:15:22.723 --> 00:15:24.583
currency dealer working for the

00:15:24.583 --> 00:15:27.103
SS called Friedrich Schwend.

00:15:28.003 --> 00:15:30.753
His operation was somewhat strange.

00:15:31.133 --> 00:15:33.793
He ran it from a castle in northern Italy.

00:15:34.423 --> 00:15:36.913
From there, he sent couriers out

00:15:36.923 --> 00:15:39.163
across Europe with suitcases of

00:15:39.203 --> 00:15:41.403
fake pounds, into Switzerland,

00:15:41.673 --> 00:15:44.363
Spain, Portugal, Sweden and Turkey,

00:15:44.653 --> 00:15:46.863
anywhere the notes could be exchanged

00:15:46.873 --> 00:15:49.603
for real money, or gold, or goods.

00:15:51.023 --> 00:15:53.503
This was, it turned out, pretty good

00:15:53.503 --> 00:15:55.523
business for Schwend, who took a cut

00:15:55.523 --> 00:15:57.803
of every transaction and, most likely,

00:15:58.033 --> 00:15:59.863
helped himself to more on the side.

00:16:00.563 --> 00:16:02.813
He bought weapons from partisans in

00:16:02.813 --> 00:16:04.843
Yugoslavia, who had captured them

00:16:04.843 --> 00:16:06.623
from the Allies, and sold them on.

00:16:07.113 --> 00:16:08.753
He bought luxuries for himself.

00:16:09.223 --> 00:16:11.903
He was, essentially, a man running

00:16:11.913 --> 00:16:13.993
a money-laundering business from a

00:16:14.013 --> 00:16:16.693
beautiful castle, except that the money

00:16:16.693 --> 00:16:18.713
he was laundering had been printed

00:16:18.723 --> 00:16:21.123
by prisoners in concentration camps.

00:16:22.353 --> 00:16:25.293
And some of the notes went to fund

00:16:25.313 --> 00:16:27.833
the most famous German spy of the war.

00:16:29.073 --> 00:16:32.023
In Ankara, the capital of neutral Turkey,

00:16:32.333 --> 00:16:35.363
the British ambassador had a valet, a

00:16:35.603 --> 00:16:38.743
personal servant, called Elyesa Bazna.

00:16:39.823 --> 00:16:43.653
In 1943, Bazna began photographing

00:16:43.653 --> 00:16:45.303
the secret documents that the

00:16:45.303 --> 00:16:47.503
ambassador kept in his safe, and

00:16:47.513 --> 00:16:50.223
selling the photographs to the Germans.

00:16:51.353 --> 00:16:53.578
The Germans gave him the codename

00:16:53.578 --> 00:16:56.433
of Cicero, and paid him around three

00:16:56.433 --> 00:16:58.203
hundred thousand pounds for his

00:16:58.203 --> 00:17:01.423
work, an enormous sum, enough to

00:17:01.423 --> 00:17:03.573
buy several large houses in London.

00:17:04.663 --> 00:17:07.583
Most of what he was paid with was fake.

00:17:08.623 --> 00:17:11.533
Bazna only discovered this after the war,

00:17:11.833 --> 00:17:13.983
when he tried to use his fortune to build

00:17:13.983 --> 00:17:16.693
a hotel, and the money was examined.

00:17:17.723 --> 00:17:19.543
He spent years afterwards writing

00:17:19.543 --> 00:17:21.093
letters to the West German

00:17:21.093 --> 00:17:23.073
government, asking to be compensated.

00:17:23.723 --> 00:17:24.453
He never was.

00:17:25.593 --> 00:17:27.653
Now, you might be wondering what

00:17:27.663 --> 00:17:29.173
the Bank of England was doing

00:17:29.423 --> 00:17:31.313
while all this was going on.

00:17:32.023 --> 00:17:34.503
Well, it knew something was coming.

00:17:35.203 --> 00:17:38.403
As early as November 1939, the

00:17:38.413 --> 00:17:40.423
British had intelligence that the

00:17:40.433 --> 00:17:42.453
Germans were planning to forge

00:17:42.453 --> 00:17:44.553
the pound, and the Bank was told.

00:17:45.712 --> 00:17:48.212
Its response, more or less, was that

00:17:48.212 --> 00:17:50.162
its notes were too good to copy.

00:17:51.022 --> 00:17:55.202
But in 1943, a forged note came in.

00:17:56.042 --> 00:17:58.222
It had been handed in at a British

00:17:58.232 --> 00:18:00.952
bank in Morocco, and it made its way to

00:18:00.952 --> 00:18:03.832
London, where a clerk opened his ledger

00:18:04.082 --> 00:18:06.262
to mark the serial number as paid.

00:18:07.232 --> 00:18:09.902
The number was already marked as paid.

00:18:11.119 --> 00:18:12.889
The Bank had a look at the note,

00:18:13.369 --> 00:18:15.349
and realised that its diagnosis

00:18:15.369 --> 00:18:17.249
that the notes were too hard to copy

00:18:17.809 --> 00:18:19.229
might have been a little arrogant.

00:18:20.099 --> 00:18:23.769
This was no crude forgery; it was, in

00:18:23.769 --> 00:18:25.879
the Bank's opinion, the most impressive

00:18:25.889 --> 00:18:28.089
forgery they had ever come across.

00:18:28.619 --> 00:18:30.459
The only reliable way to tell

00:18:30.459 --> 00:18:32.419
it apart was the watermark,

00:18:32.649 --> 00:18:35.176
and even that took an expert.

00:18:36.102 --> 00:18:38.989
And as for the question of how much

00:18:38.999 --> 00:18:42.049
fake currency there actually was, some

00:18:42.059 --> 00:18:44.449
retellings of this story say that by

00:18:44.449 --> 00:18:47.549
the end of the war, one in three pound

00:18:47.569 --> 00:18:50.289
notes in circulation was a German fake.

00:18:51.069 --> 00:18:51.979
There doesn't seem to be

00:18:51.979 --> 00:18:54.139
any real evidence for this.

00:18:54.599 --> 00:18:56.639
The Bank's own records show

00:18:56.639 --> 00:18:58.849
that in the whole of the war, it

00:18:58.849 --> 00:19:02.069
identified around 33,000 forged

00:19:02.069 --> 00:19:04.169
notes, most of which were German.

00:19:05.089 --> 00:19:07.079
Its estimate of how many were

00:19:07.319 --> 00:19:09.539
actually out in the world was higher,

00:19:09.919 --> 00:19:12.339
somewhere between fifteen and twenty

00:19:12.339 --> 00:19:14.509
million pounds' worth, so almost

00:19:14.539 --> 00:19:16.609
a billion pounds in today's money.

00:19:17.729 --> 00:19:19.349
It's a lot, of course, but

00:19:19.589 --> 00:19:21.079
nowhere near one in three.

00:19:21.919 --> 00:19:24.859
Now, when the British realised

00:19:24.859 --> 00:19:26.399
what the Germans had planned,

00:19:26.709 --> 00:19:28.419
they did something drastic.

00:19:29.369 --> 00:19:32.679
In April 1943, the Chancellor of the

00:19:32.679 --> 00:19:35.159
Exchequer, the British finance minister,

00:19:35.469 --> 00:19:37.889
announced that the Bank would stop

00:19:38.079 --> 00:19:40.879
issuing any note above five pounds.

00:19:41.319 --> 00:19:42.929
The ten, the twenty and the

00:19:42.969 --> 00:19:45.369
fifty: they were all withdrawn.

00:19:46.169 --> 00:19:47.839
And they stayed withdrawn

00:19:47.859 --> 00:19:49.079
for a very long time.

00:19:49.539 --> 00:19:50.929
Britain did not get a

00:19:50.989 --> 00:19:53.819
ten-pound note back until 1964.

00:19:54.189 --> 00:19:56.529
The twenty came back in 1970.

00:19:57.029 --> 00:19:58.469
The fifty-pound note did

00:19:58.469 --> 00:20:00.574
not return until 1981.

00:20:01.309 --> 00:20:04.019
The withdrawal hurt the Germans too.

00:20:04.769 --> 00:20:07.049
Nobody abroad wanted to touch a

00:20:07.049 --> 00:20:09.569
large British note any more, and the

00:20:09.569 --> 00:20:11.879
pound was becoming harder to spend.

00:20:12.459 --> 00:20:14.399
The dollar, on the other hand,

00:20:14.569 --> 00:20:16.689
was the currency everyone wanted.

00:20:18.109 --> 00:20:21.959
So, back in Sachsenhausen, the SS decided

00:20:21.959 --> 00:20:23.499
that if the prisoners could forge the

00:20:23.499 --> 00:20:26.329
pound, they could forge the dollar too.

00:20:27.519 --> 00:20:31.129
The order came in May 1944, and

00:20:31.289 --> 00:20:33.359
it was for hundred-dollar bills.

00:20:34.119 --> 00:20:36.119
This was harder still.

00:20:36.879 --> 00:20:39.009
American notes were printed in a

00:20:39.039 --> 00:20:42.129
different way, with raised ink, and

00:20:42.129 --> 00:20:44.869
the paper had tiny silk threads in it.

00:20:46.319 --> 00:20:48.939
And here the prisoners made a decision.

00:20:49.859 --> 00:20:52.219
They understood that the dollar project

00:20:52.369 --> 00:20:54.519
was the only thing keeping them alive;

00:20:54.989 --> 00:20:57.009
if they went too fast, or made it

00:20:57.009 --> 00:20:59.489
look too easy, they were expendable.

00:21:00.359 --> 00:21:02.419
So they worked slowly.

00:21:02.889 --> 00:21:04.279
They found problems that

00:21:04.279 --> 00:21:05.529
took weeks to solve.

00:21:05.949 --> 00:21:07.909
They produced samples that were

00:21:08.009 --> 00:21:10.109
almost right, but not quite.

00:21:10.829 --> 00:21:12.869
It was dangerous, because a prisoner

00:21:13.119 --> 00:21:15.649
suspected of sabotage would be shot.

00:21:16.349 --> 00:21:18.409
But it bought time.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:22.950
By February 1945, the first sheets

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:24.430
of hundred-dollar bills were

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:26.200
finally coming off the press.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:29.820
But by then, it no longer mattered.

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:32.060
The tide had turned.

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:34.710
The Russians were approaching, and

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:37.150
were less than fifty miles from Berlin.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:39.690
In the last days of February,

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:41.910
the order came to pack up.

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:44.670
The presses, the plates, the paper and

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:47.170
the prisoners were loaded up and sent

00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:50.550
south, first to Mauthausen concentration

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:53.390
camp in Austria, then to a tunnel

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:55.910
complex in the hills nearby, where

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.380
Krüger hoped to start printing again.

00:21:59.728 --> 00:22:01.238
That never happened.

00:22:01.838 --> 00:22:03.858
Instead, the prisoners were

00:22:03.878 --> 00:22:05.678
ordered to destroy the money.

00:22:06.288 --> 00:22:07.878
They burned what they could.

00:22:08.268 --> 00:22:09.798
What they couldn't burn was

00:22:09.808 --> 00:22:12.208
loaded onto lorries, driven to the

00:22:12.208 --> 00:22:14.448
shore of a small, dark Austrian

00:22:14.448 --> 00:22:16.748
lake, and pushed into the water.

00:22:18.138 --> 00:22:19.848
And then there was the question

00:22:19.878 --> 00:22:21.528
of the prisoners themselves.

00:22:22.068 --> 00:22:24.708
This was Nazi Germany, so

00:22:25.018 --> 00:22:26.718
there was no pardon or thank

00:22:26.718 --> 00:22:27.998
you for your excellent work.

00:22:29.128 --> 00:22:31.678
The men were moved in three groups,

00:22:31.818 --> 00:22:34.028
to another concentration camp, called

00:22:34.058 --> 00:22:36.898
Ebensee, where they were to be executed.

00:22:37.888 --> 00:22:39.988
The order was specific.

00:22:40.368 --> 00:22:42.898
All the prisoners were to be killed

00:22:42.958 --> 00:22:45.308
together, so that nobody could

00:22:45.318 --> 00:22:47.528
be left alive to tell the story.

00:22:48.647 --> 00:22:50.447
The first two groups arrived.

00:22:51.027 --> 00:22:53.957
The third did not, because the lorry

00:22:53.957 --> 00:22:56.857
carrying it had broken down on the road.

00:22:57.687 --> 00:23:00.637
So the guards at Ebensee waited.

00:23:01.557 --> 00:23:04.387
And while they waited, the war

00:23:04.567 --> 00:23:06.187
was collapsing around them.

00:23:07.157 --> 00:23:09.287
After two days, the guards gave

00:23:09.307 --> 00:23:11.727
up, and they moved the first two

00:23:11.727 --> 00:23:14.317
groups into the main camp, spread

00:23:14.587 --> 00:23:16.437
among thousands of other prisoners.

00:23:17.237 --> 00:23:20.467
Then the third lorry finally arrived.

00:23:21.287 --> 00:23:22.967
Its guards asked where the

00:23:22.977 --> 00:23:25.057
others were, and were told

00:23:25.307 --> 00:23:26.627
they had already been released.

00:23:27.457 --> 00:23:29.867
So they released their prisoners too.

00:23:30.477 --> 00:23:32.747
And then the guards themselves ran,

00:23:32.977 --> 00:23:34.947
before the Allied soldiers could arrive.

00:23:36.007 --> 00:23:38.427
The next morning, the 6th of May

00:23:38.597 --> 00:23:41.777
1945, American soldiers arrived at

00:23:41.807 --> 00:23:44.287
Ebensee, and liberated the prisoners.

00:23:45.737 --> 00:23:48.667
Amazingly enough, almost every man

00:23:48.887 --> 00:23:52.475
from Blocks 18 and 19 survived the war.

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:55.797
Bernhard Krüger hid for a year and

00:23:55.857 --> 00:23:58.177
a half, and then handed himself in

00:23:58.177 --> 00:24:00.947
to the British in November 1946.

00:24:01.637 --> 00:24:03.537
He was never charged with anything.

00:24:04.237 --> 00:24:06.527
Forging an enemy's currency in wartime,

00:24:06.977 --> 00:24:09.277
it turned out, was not a war crime.

00:24:10.477 --> 00:24:12.767
When he faced a German court after

00:24:12.767 --> 00:24:14.977
the war, several of his former

00:24:15.007 --> 00:24:17.247
prisoners spoke up for him, and

00:24:17.247 --> 00:24:19.307
said he had saved their lives.

00:24:20.647 --> 00:24:22.407
He went to work for Hahnemühle,

00:24:22.637 --> 00:24:24.427
the company that had supplied

00:24:24.427 --> 00:24:27.297
his paper, and he died in 1989.

00:24:28.627 --> 00:24:30.757
As for the lake, it never

00:24:30.757 --> 00:24:32.337
quite let go of its secrets.

00:24:33.377 --> 00:24:35.757
After the Stern divers brought up their

00:24:35.767 --> 00:24:39.397
boxes in 1959, the rumours started.

00:24:40.317 --> 00:24:42.357
If there were banknotes down

00:24:42.357 --> 00:24:44.177
there, what else was there?

00:24:44.757 --> 00:24:45.317
Gold?

00:24:45.947 --> 00:24:46.617
Documents?

00:24:47.047 --> 00:24:48.837
Nazi treasure of some kind?

00:24:50.067 --> 00:24:53.507
In 1963, a nineteen-year-old German

00:24:53.737 --> 00:24:56.447
went diving in Lake Toplitz to find out.

00:24:57.237 --> 00:24:59.157
He never came back, and it took

00:24:59.207 --> 00:25:01.147
four weeks to recover his body.

00:25:02.277 --> 00:25:05.267
Afterwards the Austrian authorities banned

00:25:05.277 --> 00:25:07.667
diving in the lake for twenty years.

00:25:08.787 --> 00:25:11.707
In 2000, an American company brought

00:25:11.717 --> 00:25:13.577
a small submarine to the lake,

00:25:13.807 --> 00:25:15.917
with a television crew and one of

00:25:15.917 --> 00:25:17.947
the former forgers, then in his

00:25:17.967 --> 00:25:19.887
eighties, watching from the shore.

00:25:20.617 --> 00:25:22.941
They brought up more banknotes.

00:25:23.707 --> 00:25:25.477
After fifty-five years in the water,

00:25:25.827 --> 00:25:27.507
they were so soft that they had

00:25:27.527 --> 00:25:29.477
to be sent to specialists to be

00:25:29.477 --> 00:25:32.367
preserved, but the former prisoner,

00:25:32.677 --> 00:25:35.337
a man named Adolf Burger, looked at

00:25:35.357 --> 00:25:37.747
them and recognised his own work.

00:25:38.837 --> 00:25:41.467
There have been more expeditions since.

00:25:41.967 --> 00:25:44.587
They have found rockets, torpedo parts,

00:25:44.917 --> 00:25:46.557
what appeared to be the wreck of a

00:25:46.557 --> 00:25:49.747
seaplane, and a box of beer bottle caps

00:25:50.007 --> 00:25:52.177
that somebody dropped in as a joke.

00:25:52.837 --> 00:25:55.430
And, of course, more banknotes.

00:25:55.737 --> 00:25:58.277
Millions of pounds of them, printed

00:25:58.327 --> 00:26:01.097
to perfection by men who expected

00:26:01.147 --> 00:26:02.987
to die for it, sitting in the

00:26:02.997 --> 00:26:05.057
dark at the bottom of an Austrian

00:26:05.057 --> 00:26:07.987
lake, and worth exactly nothing.

00:26:09.727 --> 00:26:11.907
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:26:11.907 --> 00:26:14.587
episode on Operation Bernhard, and

00:26:14.587 --> 00:26:16.997
the Nazi plan to bankrupt Britain.

00:26:17.297 --> 00:26:18.617
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:26:18.667 --> 00:26:20.617
and that you've learnt something new.

00:26:21.187 --> 00:26:23.327
You've been listening to English Learning

00:26:23.327 --> 00:26:26.027
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:26:26.357 --> 00:26:29.487
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:26:29.497 --> 00:26:31.497
I'll catch you in the next episode.

